### Analysis of System-Level Proximity Relationships for Power Screwdriver Components
To address the query, we examine the connections between the specified power screwdriver components: Bit, Transmission, Motor, Electrical System, Battery Holder, Housing, and External Environment. Each component's interaction signifies a critical aspect of the screwdriver's functionality and design. The Bit is directly connected to the Transmission, indicating a mechanical interaction for torque application. The Transmission, in turn, interfaces with the Motor, showcasing the transformation of electrical energy into mechanical motion. The Motor’s function is critically dependent on the Electrical System for power, which also connects to the Battery Holder, indicating a supply route for electric energy. The Housing encompasses and protects all internal components, asserting a structural relationship with each. The External Environment impacts the Housing primarily, but it also has indirect effects on other components through thermal and physical stress. This analysis forms the basis of the Design Structure Matrix (DSM).
